## Graphical abstract
## Highlights
(1). Heterometallic CoNi-MOFs was formed through hydrothermal synthesis method to improve the performance of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ .   
(2). Experiments show that the addition of $C0^{2+}$ in MOF can increase the photocurrent density while the addition of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ can decrease the onset potential. (3). Composite photocatalytic materials have excellent stability as well as high performance.
Abstract
In this work, an efficient nanoporous ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ composite photoelectrode with high photocurrent and low onset potential for photoelectrochemical water splitting is achieved through modification of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ with a stable heterometallic CoNi-MOF layer. Compared with pristine ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ anode, the formation of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 increases the photocurrent density by about $260\%$ . In addition, the CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode is of greater steadiness for water oxidation, without insignificant activity attenuation within 3 reaction hours at $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE. It is postulated that bimetalorganic framework CoNi-MOFs could supply more open sites which account for the impressively enhanced PEC performance of BiVO4 photoanodes toward water splitting under illumination. The results demonstrate a new method to fabricate dual metal nanoparallelepiped-like MOF for steady hydrolysis photoanode material.
Keywords: CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4; Photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting; stability
## 1. Introduction
Due to current energy crisis and environmental pollution, the demand of human beings to explore sustainable and clean energy for replacement of fossil fuel is becoming increasingly huge.1-6 Photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting, driven by solar energy, is widely studied as one of the green channels to produce hydrogen fuel by harvesting sunlight.7-11 Among multitudinous star semiconductors, ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ is the leader in this field owning to its suitable band gap $(\sim2.4\mathrm{eV})$ , adequate positive valence band edge (VB, ${\sim}2.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), earth-abundance and outstanding PEC stability.12-14 Even though, there are still some inherent drawbacks for ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ photoanode in PEC water splitting, such as poor electron mobility, severe electron–hole recombination and sluggish surface water oxidation reaction.15-17 To achieve efficient PEC water splitting, it is crucial to overcome the surface electron–hole recombination of the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ photoanodes.18-20
Thanks to their large specific surface area and tunable pore structure, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have aroused intensive interest in a wide range of applications,21- 23 e.g., gas storage and separation,24 proton conduction25 as well as chemical sensors.26 Many MOFs derived from divalent metal, such as $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , possess outstanding porosity and can be used in various applications.27-29 Recently, increasingly more researchers are beginning to combine MOFs with optical semiconductors to produce materials with excellent photoelectric behavior. However, many of them suffer from instable issues.30-32 For instance, Wang et al. investigated the stability of the zeolitic imidazolate skeleton-8 (ZIF-8) in seven common anionic aqueous solutions.33 Ding et al. demonstrated the metal organic framework MIL-53(Fe) could serve as an efficient hole-transfer cocatalyst for significantly improving the PEC performance of Mo doped ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ photoanodes under solar irradiation.34 Nevertheless, their stability is deficient. In addition, our previous work suggested Co-MIm was an effective cocatalyst as well to improve the PCE performance of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ but suffered unsatisfactory stability.35 The unsteadiness of many MOFs in solution has considerably limited their further application and commercialization.
As we know, high valence metal ions can greatly improve the stability of MOF, and the reversible reaction between ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ makes it a potential anode catalytic material.36-38 Because Ni and Co can easily replace H atoms in ligands, we have designed a simple strategy to synthesize a series of 3D bimetallic MOFs (scheme 1). By optimizing the molar ratio of Co and Ni, these MOFs can be directly used in OER reaction. On the basis of above analyses, a bismuth vanadate photoanode was prepared using electrodeposition-calcination method, followed by the hydrothermal deposition of CoNi-MOFs nano-structure to construct a binary photoanode, which ultimately lead to excellent PEC performance owing to the promoted light harvesting capability, enhanced charge separation as well as injection efficiency and good stability. In addition, the obtained photoanode showed sterling ability in the oxidation of water into oxygen under AM 1.5G light irradiation, achieving a photocurrent density of $3.2~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23{\mathrm{V}}$ vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Reagents and materials
Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) coated glass as substrates were purchased from Zhuhai Kaivo Optoelectronic Technology Co., Ltd, ${<}15~\mathrm{{X/sq}}$ . China. Nickel (Ⅱ) chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(NiCl_{2}{\bullet\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $99.0\%$ ) was obtained from Shanghai Zhongtai Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Cobalt chloride hexahydrate( $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99.0\%$ ) was purchased from Energy Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.0\%\rangle$ , Barium nitrate pentahydrate $(\mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\bullet}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99.0\%$ ), potassium iodide (KI, $99.0\%$ ), Anhydrous ethanol were bought from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. P-benzoquinone $(99.0\%)$ was purchased from Tianjin Institute of Fine Chemicals. 2- methylimidazole $(98.0\%)$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p}}$ -phthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\tilde{C}8H_{6}O_{4}}$ , $98.5\%$ ) were obtained from Tianjin
Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute. Dimethyl sulfoxide was purchased from Beijing Chemical Works. All chemicals were used directly without any further purification. Deionized water was used in all reactions.
## 2.2. Synthesis of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4, Co-MOF/BiVO4 and NiMOF/BiVO4 photoanodes
BiVO4 electrode was synthesized by a previously reported method (Supporting Information).35 $32~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, $2\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and $2\mathrm{mL}$ of water were mixed in a 100 mL beakers. Next, 0.75 mmol of p-phthalic acid was dissolved in the mixed solution under the action of ultrasonic waves. Subsequently, ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ having the same molar ratio were added. After that, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of the mixed solution was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon container, and the conductive surface of the prepared ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ photoanode was placed inclined downwards at $45^{\circ}$ , heated at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an airtight condition for 48 hours. Finally, the reacted photoanode was washed three times with deionized water and ethanol, and the electrode obtained after drying was referred to as CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode. The fabrication process of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode is illustrated in following Scheme 1. The experimental operations of synthesis of Co-MOF/BiVO4 and Ni-MOF/BiVO4 photoanodes are the same as the preparation of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4. Except that $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.375 mmol) and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.375 mmol) were replaced by $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.75 mmol) or $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.75 mmol).
## 2.3. Synthesis of CoNi-MOFs, Co-MOF and Ni-MOF powders
CoNi-MOFs powders were prepared by the same method as above. DMF ( $32{\mathrm{~mL}}$ , ethanol $2\mathrm{mL}$ ) and water $2\mathrm{mL}$ ) were mixed in a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker. Next, 0.75 mmol pphthalic acid was dissolved into the mixed solution under ultrasonication. Subsequently, ${\mathrm{CoCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\mathrm{NiCl}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with the same molar ratio were added. Then, the mixed solution was transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon vessel at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ under airtight conditions. Finally, the obtained powder was collected by centrifugation and washed by deionized water and ethanol for several times. The experimental operations of synthesis of Co-MOF and Ni-MOF powders are the same as the preparation of CoNiMOFs. Except that $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.375 mmol) and $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.375 mmol) were replaced by $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.75 mmol) or $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (0.75 mmol).
## 2.4. Characterization of material structure and properties
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements were characterized by JSM6701E at an accelerating voltage of $5\mathrm{\kV.}$ Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurements were carried out by using JYOL-2010 microscope operated at $200\mathrm{kV}.$ The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were measured on RigaKu X ray diffractometer D/MAX-2200/PC equipped with Cu Kα radiation (40 thousand volts, 20 mA). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy analysis of the samples were carried out on Nicolet NEXUS 670 spectrometer in the range of $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The SHIMADZU X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out in the PHI5702 photoelectron spectrometer, and the monochromatic Al Kalph (150 W) was used as the excitation source of X-ray. UV-visible diffuse reflectance spectra were performed on a UV-3100 spectrometer by using BaSO4 as reference. Evolved hydrogen gas was detected by a gas chromatograph (GC-9560).
## 2.5. PEC characterization and photoelectrochemical hydrogen production
All photoanodes were measured as working electrodes with an area of $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ on an electrochemical workstation (CHI660E, Shanghai Zhenghua). The three-electrode configuration using Pt foil and Ag/AgCl electrode as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively was adopted for PEC measurements in electrolyte of $0.5{\textbf{M}}$ $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ . Linear sweep voltammograms (LSV) at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and transient response under chopped light are carried out under AM 1.5G illumination with intensity of $100\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
The Faradaic efficiency of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ [Farad $\left(\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)\dot{}$ ] and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [Farad $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ ] evolution on the CoNiMOFs/BiVO4 film in 0.5 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE were determined using gas chromatography. Before testing, the dissolved oxygen in the $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte was purged by ${\bf N}_{2}$ -bubbling about 30 min. A GC-9560 gas chromatograph was used to detect the amount of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. [Farad $\left(\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)^{\cdot}$ ] and [Farad $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)]$ were calculated with the following equation.
$\mathrm{Farad}(\%)({\cal O}_{2})={\frac{(a m o u n t o f{\cal O}_{2}e v o l u t i o n)}{(t h e o r e t i c a l a m o u n t o f{\cal O}_{2}e v o l u t i o n)}}\times100$ $\mathrm{Farad}(\%)(H_{2})=\frac{(a m o u n t o f H_{2}~e v o l u t i o n)}{(t h e o r e t i c a l~a m o u n t o f H_{2}~e v o l u t i o n)}\times100\$
## 2.6. Determination of photoelectric efficiency
The incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) of each wavelength was tested by irradiation with a 300 W xenon lamp with monochromator. The IPCE value was calculated by the equation
$$
\mathrm{IPCE}(\%)=\frac{1240\times J\left(m A/c m^{2}\right)}{\lambda\left(\mathrm{nm}\right)\times\mathrm{P\left(mW/cm^{2}\right)}}\times100\%
$$
Where J is the photocurrent density at a specific wavelength, $\mathrm{\bfP}$ is the measured light power density, and $\lambda$ is the wavelength of incident light.
The charge injection efficiency and the charge separation efficiency can be determined as follows:
Where Jwater and Jsulfite is the photocurrent density for PEC water oxidation and sulfite oxidation, respectively. $\mathbf{J_{\mathrm{{abs}}}}$ is the photocurrent density when completely converting the absorbed photons into current $(\mathrm{APCE}=100\%)$
The following equation is used to calculate the applied bias photonto-current efficiency (ABPE) from the LSV curve, where J is the photocurrent density at the potential of $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{bias}}$ and $\mathrm{\bfP}$ is the simulated solar light intensity. All measured potentials were transformed into RHE $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{RHE}}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}+0.059\mathrm{V}\times\mathrm{pH}+0.197\mathrm{V}).$
$$
\mathrm{ABPE}(\%)=\left[\frac{J(m A/c m^{2})\times(1.23-V_{b i a s})(V)}{P(m W/c m^{2})}\right]\times100\%
$$
The absorbed photon-to-current efficiency (APCE) was obtained by dividing the IPCE by the light harvesting efficiency (LHE) using following equations:
$$
\mathrm{APCE}=I P C E/L H E
$$
$\mathrm{LHE}=1-10^{-(\lambda)}$ ( $(\lambda)$ is the absorbance at wavelength $\lambda$ )
## 3. Results and Discussion
Scheme 1 illustrates the synthesis method of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode. Firstly, the pure BiVO4 grown on an FTO substrate was prepared through an electrodeposition method in which BiOI electrodes were synthesized as precursors and converted into ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ by adding the $\mathrm{V}^{5+}$ source dropwise during the calcination process. Then, the CoNi-MOFs were deposited on the BiVO4 samples via a simple hydrothermal method. The structural characterization of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 is shown in the Scheme 1. The composite photoanodes were prepared with various contents (5, 10 and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ ) of hydrothermal solution, respectively.
The nanostructure of the as-synthesized photoanode was examined by microscopic observation. SEM images showed that the nanoporous BiVO4 consisted with the morphology of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ in the previous literature,39 illustrating that the nanoporous ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ was successfully grown on the FTO substrate (Figure 1a). Figure 1b shows that the newly-coated CoNi-MOFs had a nanoparallelepiped-like structure, covering the original nanoporous ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ . The EDS element mapping profiled in Figure 1c-i revealed that CoNi-MOFs were homogeneously deposited on the BiVO4 surface. In addition, the CoNi-MOFs on the composite electrode were consistent with the morphology of the prepared MOF powder (Figure S1). The elemental composition of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 composite electrode was determined by EDS and illustrated in Figure S2, which confirmed the existence of Bi, V, O, Co and Ni entities, and the good agreement of atomic ratios of Co and Ni to their stoichiometric ratios ${\bf(C o:N i\approx1:1)}$ ). In order to investigate the effect of cobalt-nickel metal ratio on the structure of MOF, scanning electron microscopy was carried out for MOF with different bimetallic ratios. It can be seen from Figure S3a-f that the thickness of bimetallic MOF became thicker as the proportion of cobalt metal increased gradually.
To characterize the structure and the crystalline quality of the CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode, transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and EDS analyses were performed. The TEM image of the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ is shown in Figure 2a, pure BiVO4 showed a porous morphology, which was consistent with the SEM results reported above. As observed in Figure 2b, CoNi-MOFs layer was coated on the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ particles. The morphology of the CoNi-MOFs was agreed well with the SEM results. The EDS element mapping profiles in Figure 2c revealed that CoNi-MOFs were deposited on the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ surface. Comparing with previous studies, the Co and Ni atoms in this structure coordinated with six oxygen atoms (Figure 2d). Figure S4 was magnified crystal structure of CoNi-MOFs, which is the same as Figure 2d.
In order to identify the crystalline phase of the materials, XRD patterns collected from the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ , CoNi-MOFs and CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 samples were shown in Figure 3a. The result revealed that BiVO4 should belong to a monoclinic structure corresponding to JCPDS No. 14-0688 and the FTO substrate consistent with JCPDS No. 46-1088.40 The evaluation above is the same as the white tungsten-type phase and zircon-type phase that both possess remarkable photocatalytic activities. To further confirm the existence of CoNi-MOFs, we prepared CoNi-MOFs powder under the same experimental conditions for XRD measurements. Comparing with related literature,41 it expectedly showed three peaks assigned to the (200), (001), and ( ̅2 01) crystallographic planes of CoNi-MOFs, respectively. Corresponding to the CoNi
MOFs/BiVO4 photoanodes, we found the existence of (200) crystal planes CoNi-MOFs, which indicated that we have successfully prepared CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4. In order to present a convincing illustration, we also synthesized single metal Co-MOF and NiMOF to conduct a further comparison (Figure S5a-c). It can be seen from the XRD pattern that the obtained single metal MOF had the same crystalline phase as the bimetallic MOF.
The FT-IR spectra (Figure 3c) demonstrated that the para-aromatic CH groups were observed in CoNi-MOFs and CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4. The two peaks at 815.5 and 749.2 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were characteristic of the para-aromatic $\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching bands. Two intensive peaks in 1566 and $1364~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{as}}$ 1 $-\mathrm{COO)}$ and $\mathrm{V_{s}}$ (−COO) of terephthalate anions, respectively.42 These results agreed well with those of the previous report.43 The FT-IR peaks of all the samples were almost the same, which confirmed that they were isostructural. UV-vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy was used to study the optical properties of the samples. In Figure S6, the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ showed a typical absorbance onset at around ${510}\mathrm{nm}$ , and the calculated band gap of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ was around $2.4~\mathrm{eV},$ which closed to the value of the monoclinic phase reported.44 The absorption intensity of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 before $380~\mathrm{nm}$ was higher than ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ , which might be beneficial to the performance of PEC. In addition, we can see that there is no difference between the TEM, XRD and IR spectra (Figure S7, 3b, 3d) of the composite sample before and after the PEC test, indicating that the composite structure after the photoelectric performance test has been unchanged.
The chemical bonding state of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 was characterized using XPS, and the results were shown in Figure 4. The full spectrum confirmed the existence of Co, Ni, Bi, V and O elements (Figure 4a), matching well with the EDX elemental mapping result (Figure 2d). Figure 4b reveals that O 1s of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 can be split into two peaks: 529.6 and 530.4 V, which are attributed to lattice oxygen $\mathrm{(O_{L})}$ and chemisorbed or dissociated oxygen species $(\mathrm{Oc})$ , respectively.45-47 Figure 4c shows that the peaks located at around 781.1 and $797.2\mathrm{eV}$ were indexed to $\mathrm{Co}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively, and the other two peaks centered at 786.9 and $803.3{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~eV}}}$ respectively corresponded to shake-up satellites, which were the characteristic peaks of the Co (II) oxidation state.48 In the high-resolution Ni 2p spectrum (Figure 4d), the fitting peaks at 855.8 and $873.7{\mathrm{~eV}}$ were attributed to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ spin orbits, respectively. Additionally, the two broad peaks at 861.7 and $880.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ were identified as shake-up satellites (‘‘Sat.’’) of Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. The results above displayed the characteristic bands of the Ni (II) oxidation state.49 These results demonstrated that Ni and Co ions were predominantly in the valence state of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ in the assynthesized CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 sample. In addition, as showed in Figure S8a-b, peaks at 159.1 and $164.3\mathrm{eV}$ were attributable to $\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}$ , and those at 516.6 and $523.9\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to V5+.50-51
The PEC water splitting activity of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 was measured under AM 1.5G illumination in 0.5 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. In order to explore the effect of CoNiMOFs load on the PEC performance of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ film, the Figure S9 shows the solution of CoNi-MOFs deposition was divided into 5, 10 and $15~\mathrm{mL}$ . It can be seen the photoanode prepared with $5~\mathrm{mL}$ hydrothermal solution had the highest photocurrent.
We can also see from the LSV of Figure S10 and Figure S11 that when the ratio of cobalt to nickel was 1:1, the photoelectric performance was the best. In Figure S10, we can find that single Ni-MOF could reduce the starting point of the composite electrode, while Co-MOF can improve its photocurrent. The linear current-voltage curves of the electrodes were shown in Figure 5a. Pure BiVO4 showed a $1.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ photocurrent at $1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. However, the photocurrent densities of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 reached to ca. $3.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE, which was almost 3 times as high as that of pure BiVO4. Moreover, compared with the previous reports of Co/Ni-based photoanode materials in Table 1, the CoNi-MOFs exhibits excellent PEC performance, which was attributed to 3D nanostructure. The improved photocurrent of deposited CoNi-MOFs was due to the improved interface of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 catalyst that brought about effective charge separation and transport. The onset potential was defined as the potential at the intersection point of the tangent at the maximum slope of photocurrent and the dark current curve.52 The photocurrent onset potential of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ here was similar to those reported previously.53 Compared to the pure BiVO4, the onset potential of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 significantly reduced from 0.71 V to $0.58\mathrm{V}$ with a cathodic shift of $130\mathrm{mV}.$ Furthermore, the onset potential of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 was lower than that of pure BiVO4 photoanode without illumination (Figure 5b). The chopping photocurrent density-voltage image showed the same condition (Figure S12). Moreover, the chopped-light chronoamperometry (Figure 5c) was taken to further identify the transfer kinetics of the photocarriers. Although there was some fast decay of the photocurrent after sudden illumination because of the competition between the fast carrier generation and slow surface oxidation dynamics, the decay of CoNiMOFs/BiVO4 was obviously smaller than pure BiVO4. Furthermore, the steady-state photocurrent of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 at $1.23\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE was 3 times larger than ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ , which was consistent with the LSV shown in Figure 5a. It can be seen that a transient current was observed for both CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 and pure BiVO4 when removing the illumination, which can be attributed to the accumulated charges at the surface due to the surface of trapping states. Therefore, it is necessary to join catalyst on bismuth vanadate electrode to promote the participation of hole in water oxidation reaction.
Moreover, in the electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS), the arc radius of Nyquist plots can be used to reflect the electron transfer resistance $\mathbf{\Gamma}(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}})$ at photoelectrode/electrolyte interface between different samples, and the smaller arc radius means lower $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ .54 As shown in Figure 5d, the $\mathrm{BiVO_{4}}$ photoanode exhibited a large semicircle under irradiation, which displayed a high interface charge transfer barrier. Meanwhile a much smaller $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ was observed for the CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 film compared to the BiVO4 films. The same result under non-irradiation further ascertains the promotion of the charge transfer by CoNi-MOFs across the electrolyte/semiconductor interface.
The charge injection efficiency was investigated using the ratio of the holescavenger-assisted (sulfite-oxidation) current to the water-oxidation current. The LSV curves of the ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ and CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanodes measured with ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S O}_{3}$ in the electrolyte were shown in Figure S13. With the addition of ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S O}_{3}$ in the electrolyte, the LSV curves of the photoanodes showed increment in the photocurrent densities comparing to the LSV curves without ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S O}_{3}$ . The charge injection and separation efficiencies of the pure ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ and CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanodes as functions of the 1.23 V vs. RHE were shown in Figure 6a and b, respectively. The charge injection efficiency of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 was found higher than that of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ in the whole potential range. At 1.23 V vs. RHE, surface for CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 attained $66.3\%$ , while that of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ was $33.4\%$ . This charge injection efficiency showed that the surface charge transfer was enhanced significantly and the surface charge recombination was suppressed effectively.55 The charge separation efficiency also showed the same result as the charge injection efficiency.56 The incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) value is a critical index to study light conversion efficiency (Figure 6c), which exhibits that CoNi-MOFs catalyst could obviously promote the IPCE value, i.e. that of CoNiMOFs/BiVO4 was up to $31\%$ at $350-470\mathrm{nm}$ , about three times larger than pure BiVO4. The absorbed photon-to-current efficiency (APCE) is the dividing value of IPCE and LHE, the LHE graph was displayed in Figure S14.57 APCE of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 at $430\mathrm{nm}$ was $39\%$ , about 4 times as high as that of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ (Figure 6d). The APCE results indicated that the composite photoanode may utilize the absorbed light more efficiently. Additionally, the applied bias photon to current efficiency (ABPE) was $0.65\%$ for the CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 at $0.6\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE while only $0.11\%$ found for pure BiVO4 (Figure S15).
To verify the stability of the CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 photoanode, a long term i−t measurement was performed. As seen in Figure 7a, the pure BiVO4 lost half of its initial photocurrent within $3\ \mathrm{~h~}$ , while the photocurrent densities of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 remained relatively stable throughout the process, retaining $90\%$ of their initial currents at the end of 10 800 s. The generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gases by CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 were tested by gas chromatography (Figure 7b). The ratio of evolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is close to the theoretical value. In addition, the Faradaic efficiencies of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ evolution reaction were about $90\%$ . So, it illustrated that most of the photo-generated charges were joined for $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production in this system.
Based on the above discussions, a possible mechanism of CoNi-MOFs enhanced the water splitting performance of ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ electrodes was proposed (Figure 8). With light irradiation, ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ absorbed photons and thus produced electrons and holes in the conduction band and valence band. The holes were then migrated to and consequently stored in CoNi-MOFs, where ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ were oxidized to a higher valence state like $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}/\mathrm{Co}^{4+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{3+}/N i^{4+}}$ via the capture of holes. Then high valence metal ions subsequently acted as active sites to oxidize the interfacial $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ of CoNi-MOFs to generate $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , while electrons were transferred to $\mathrm{Pt}$ counter electrode via external circuit and participated in water reduction reaction to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have developed CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 as photoanode for PEC water splitting via a facile hydrothermal deposition method. The nanogeometry CoNi-MOFs served as cocatalyst joining water oxidation reactions. This structure greatly increases the surface reaction sites and significantly improves superior PEC water oxidation performance. The photocurrent of CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 was received as $3.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at 1.23 V vs. RHE for water oxidation, which is ca. 3 times higher than that of pure ${\bf B i V O_{4}}$ .
It is also the binary CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 that exhibits outstanding stability compared to that of pure BiVO4. This CoNi-MOFs/BiVO4 work has shown that bimetal MOF as a cocatalyst has a targeted and effective modification to the traditional PEC photoanode system, which can open a new way for the design of high performance PEC photoanodes.
